{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Poitiers and the ruler of the County of Tripoli. He was the son of Bohemond III of Antioch and his wife Orgueilleuse of Hauteville. Raymond III was a significant figure in the history of the Crusader states in the Levant.\n\n* **Early Life and Succession*** \nRaymond III was born around 1150 AD. He succeeded his father Bohemond III in 1201 AD. Raymond III married Eschiva of Armenia but did not have any children with her. \n\nAfter the death of his first wife Eschiva of Armenia he married Alice of Armenia and had two children with her. His first child was Maria of Antioch and his second child was Bohemond IV of Antioch and Tripoli.\n\n* **Reign and Military Campaigns*** \nDuring his reign Raymond III had to face the threat of the Ayyubid dynasty. He fought against the Ayyubid forces in several battles. Raymond III also had to deal with the internal conflicts within the County of Tripoli. He had to balance the power of the nobles and the influence of the Church.\n\n* **Later Life and Death*** \nRaymond III died in 1187 AD. He was succeeded by his son Bohemond IV of Antioch and Tripoli. The County of Tripoli continued to play a significant role in the history of the Crusader states in the Levant.\n\nRaymond III's reign was marked by his efforts to defend the County of Tripoli against the Ayyubid forces. He also had to navigate the complex web of alliances and rivalries within the Crusader states. Despite the challenges he faced Raymond III played an important role in the history of the region.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.014390614}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.00016938205}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0010648064}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.1300828}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.349725e-05}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.00021371941}, {"token": "oli", "logprob